#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a47ce42d-7fbb-5f7c-999d-4c276632310b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"}] :question/id #uuid "5fb13d73-4955-5ae4-bb31-bec3aef3cca9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13237" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template below?\n\n> **Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1$.\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $000000$ we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n> **Conclusion**: Therefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n> for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$." :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1)$. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n> **Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1$.\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some\n\n$$\nn=k \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n)$.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k \\geq 1}$, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. We start by writing down $P(k+1)$.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$, the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n> **Conclusion**: Therefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n> for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6befd731-de3f-545c-aca4-b51b67c68ace" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Matrix Identities"}] :question/id #uuid "d0ade273-b9ef-5c2d-bdcc-3f56f95e79ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-13238" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots$, where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1)$. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\n> We proceed using induction on $n$.\n> **Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1$.\n> For the left-hand side, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$,\n> and for the right-hand side, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4^{1} - 1 & 4^{1}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n> Thus, our statement is true for $n=1$.\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some\n\n$$\nn=k\\geq 1\n$$\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1$, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. Let's start by writing down $P(k+1)$. This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\n> We write\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^{k + 1}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^{k} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n> By the inductive hypothesis, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^{k} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4^{k} - 1 & 4^{k}\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4^{k} - 1 + 3 \\cdot 4^{k} & 4 \\cdot 4^{k}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 \\cdot 4^{k} - 1 & 4 \\cdot 4^{k}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4^{k + 1} - 1 & 4^{k + 1}\\end{bmatrix}$,\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, by the principle of induction,\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n> is true for all $n\\geq 1$."}]}
